<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">

  <title>Add and Subtract Polynomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m81383</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Add and Subtract Polynomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c82f19a0-7d76-445d-a89f-9c230a8337f5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>

  <content>
    <section>
      <para>In this lesson, you learned how to:</para>
      <list>
        <item>Determine the degree of polynomials.</item>
        <item>Add and subtract polynomials.</item>
        <item>Evaluate a polynomial function for a given value.</item>
        <item>Add and subtract polynomial functions.</item>
      </list>
      <para>Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that helped you reach those goals:</para>
      <list bullet-style="none">
        <item>6.1.1: Understanding Polynomials</item>
        <item>
          <list>
            <item> In this activity, you classified different types of polynomials. You also found
          the degree of different polynomials.</item>
          </list>
        </item>
        <item>6.1.2: Adding and Subtracting Polynomials</item>
        <item>
          <list>
            <item> In this activity, you added and subtracted different types of polynomials by
          grouping like terms.</item>
            <item> 6.1.2: Self Check</item>
            <item>6.1.2: Additional Resources</item>
          </list>
        </item>
        <item>6.1.3: Evaluating a Polynomial Function for a Given Value</item>
        <item>
          <list>
            <item> In this activity, you substituted given values into various polynomial functions
          and simplified to find solutions.</item>
            <item> 6.1.3: Self Check </item>
            <item>6.1.3: Additional Resources</item>
          </list>
        </item>
        <item>6.1.4: Adding and Subtracting Polynomial Functions</item>
        <item>
          <list>
            <item>In this activity, you used the skills learned in previous activities to add and
          subtract polynomial functions. You were able to compare your work by evaluating the
          original and new polynomial functions using the same value.</item>
            <item> 6.1.4: Self Check </item>
            <item>6.1.4: Additional Resources</item>
          </list>
        </item>
        <item>6.1.5: Reviewing Polynomial Addition and Subtraction</item>
        <item>
          <list>
            <item>In this activity, you reviewed all of the skills you learned adding and
          subtracting polynomials to solve a tic-tac-toe puzzle.</item>
          </list>
        </item>
      </list>
      <para>After these activities, you completed the following <emphasis>practice</emphasis>:</para>
      <list bullet-style="none">
        <item>6.1.6: Practice</item>
      </list>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>In this unit, remember to double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge,
        if needed.</para>
      <para>A <term>monomial</term> is an algebraic expression with exactly one term: <math
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
          <mrow>
            <mn>5</mn>
            <msup>
              <mi>x</mi>
              <mn>2</mn>
            </msup>
          </mrow>
        </math></para>
      <para>A <term>binomial</term> is an algebraic expression with exactly two terms: <math
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
          <mrow>
            <mn>5</mn>
            <msup>
              <mi>x</mi>
              <mn>2</mn>
            </msup>
            <mo>+</mo>
            <mn>6</mn>
            <msup>
              <mi>y</mi>
              <mn>3</mn>
            </msup>
          </mrow>
        </math></para>
      <para>A <term>trinomial</term> is an algebraic expression with exactly three terms: <math
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
          <mrow>
            <mn>5</mn>
            <msup>
              <mi>x</mi>
              <mn>2</mn>
            </msup>
            <mo>+</mo>
            <mn>6</mn>
            <msup>
              <mi>y</mi>
              <mn>3</mn>
            </msup>
            <mo>−</mo>
            <mn>7</mn>
            <mi>c</mi>
          </mrow>
        </math></para>
      <para>Monomials, binomials, and trinomials are all different types of polynomials. A <term>
        polynomial</term> is a monomial or two or more monomials combined by addition or
        subtraction.</para>
      <para>The <term>degree of a polynomial</term> is the highest degree of all its terms.</para>
      <div class="os-raise-usermessage-link">
        <para>Log into <link url="https://student.desmos.com">student.desmos.com</link> using the
        information provided by your teacher to complete the activity.</para>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>We will solve these two problems as a class.</para>

      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
              <item>Do these terms have the same variables with the same exponents? Explain your
                answer. <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"
                  display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>x</mi>
                      <mn>4</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>y</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                  </mrow>
                </math><math
                  xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>x</mi>
                      <mn>4</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>y</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Yes. Both terms have the same variables raised to the same degrees: <math
                xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mi>x</mi>
                </mrow>
              </math> is raised to the fourth
              power, and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mi>y</mi>
                </mrow>
              </math> is squared.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>

      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <list type="enumerated">
              <item> Add the terms together: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"
                  display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>x</mi>
                      <mn>4</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>y</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>x</mi>
                      <mn>4</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>y</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                  </mrow>
                </math>.
                What is the sum? </item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work. </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>14</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>x</mi>
                    <mn>4</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>y</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>

      <para>In problems 3 - 7, simplify the monomial addition and subtraction problems on your own.
        Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work.</para>

      <!--Q#3-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <list type="enumerated">
              <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>12</mn>
                    <mi>q</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>9</mn>
                    <mi>q</mi>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>21</mn>
                  <mi>q</mi>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->

      <!--Q#4-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <list type="enumerated">
              <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>14</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>c</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>5</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>c</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>9</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>c</mi>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->

      <!--Q#5-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <list type="enumerated">
              <item>
                <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>g</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>h</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>g</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>h</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>g</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>h</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->

      <!--Q#6-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <list type="enumerated">
              <item>
                <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <mi>m</mi>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>n</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mi>p</mi>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>5</mn>
                    <mi>m</mi>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>n</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mi>p</mi>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>13</mn>
                  <mi>m</mi>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>n</mi>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mi>p</mi>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->

      <!--Q#7-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <list type="enumerated">
              <item>
                <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>16</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>a</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>b</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>a</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>8</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>a</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>b</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->

      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <div class="os-raise-ib-input" data-button-text="Solution"
        data-content-id="52fdbc4d-81fd-4d07-a13e-40a06038bc34" data-fire-event="eventShow"
        data-schema-version="1.0">
        <div class="os-raise-ib-input-content">
          <list type="enumerated">
            <item> Can the following monomials be simplified using like terms? Explain. <math
                xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>17</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>a</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>c</mi>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>6</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>a</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>b</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>c</mi>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></item>
          </list>
        </div>
        <div
          class="os-raise-ib-input-prompt">
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </div>
        <div class="os-raise-ib-input-ack">
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>No, the monomial terms do not contain the same
        variables with the same exponents. The first term has the variable <math
              xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mi>b</mi>
              </mrow>
            </math> with an exponent of 1. The second
        term has the variable <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mi>b</mi>
              </mrow>
            </math> with an exponent of 2. The
        monomials are not like terms.</para>
        </div>
      </div>

      <para>In problems 9 - 13, simplify the polynomial addition and subtraction problems on your
        own. Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work.</para>

      <!--Q#9-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <list type="enumerated">
              <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>5</mn>
                    <mi>k</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>7</mn>
                    <mi>m</mi>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>7</mn>
                    <mi>k</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>9</mn>
                    <mi>m</mi>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>4</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>You can use the following video if you need some help.</para>
            <media alt="...">
              <iframe src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ba5bc330fb9f7f706e35cf5a38cdfedb85f34aec"/>
                    <!-- <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On"
                      src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/712187a19365bfc0825bd798bf9f258c6ec2c971"
                      srclang="en_us">
                    </track> -->
            </media>

            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>12</mn>
                  <mi>k</mi>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>16</mn>
                  <mi>m</mi>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>6</mn>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->


      <!--Q#10-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
              <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>12</mn>
                    <mi>w</mi>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>11</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>5</mn>
                    <mi>w</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>7</mn>
                  <mi>w</mi>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>5</mn>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->


      <!--Q#11-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
              <item><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>9</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>h</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>4</mn>
                    <mi>h</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>17</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>h</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>9</mn>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>11</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>h</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>4</mn>
                  <mi>h</mi>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>8</mn>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->


      <!--Q#12-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
              <item>
                <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>f</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <mi>f</mi>
                    <mi>v</mi>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>v</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>f</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>v</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>f</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>6</mn>
                  <mi>f</mi>
                  <mi>v</mi>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->


      <!--Q#13-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="13" type="enumerated">
              <item>
                <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                  <mrow>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>8</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>z</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>6</mn>
                    <mi>z</mi>
                    <mi>x</mi>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>x</mi>
                      <mn>2</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                    <mo>+</mo>
                    <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                    <mn>12</mn>
                    <msup>
                      <mi>z</mi>
                      <mn>3</mn>
                    </msup>
                    <mo>−</mo>
                    <mn>4</mn>
                    <mi>z</mi>
                    <mi>x</mi>
                    <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                  </mrow>
                </math></item>
            </list>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
                <mrow>
                  <mn>20</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>z</mi>
                    <mn>3</mn>
                  </msup>
                  <mo>−</mo>
                  <mn>10</mn>
                  <mi>z</mi>
                  <mi>x</mi>
                  <mo>+</mo>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                  <msup>
                    <mi>x</mi>
                    <mn>2</mn>
                  </msup>
                </mrow>
              </math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Interaction
      End -->


      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you
      will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>

            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      </example><!--Start
      interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all
      have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the
      button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <div class="os-raise-ib-input" data-button-text="Solution"
        data-content-id="3cd1023e-3b4a-4e82-b3b2-ddd9101db872" data-schema-version="1.0"
        data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
        <div class="os-raise-ib-input-content">
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para>Simplify. Use the "^" symbol to enter an
        exponent.</para>
          <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>3</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
        </div>
        <div
          class="os-raise-ib-input-prompt">
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </div>
        <div class="os-raise-ib-input-ack">
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"
              display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
          <para>Here
        is how to solve this problem: <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"
              display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>3</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mo stretchy="false">(</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo stretchy="false">)</mo>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
          <para><emphasis>Step
        1 - </emphasis>Distribute the multiplication over addition.</para>
          <para>
            <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
          <para><emphasis>Step
        2 - </emphasis>Use the Commutative Property to rearrange like terms together.</para>
          <para>
            <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mi>b</mi>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>+</mo>
                <mn>2</mn>
                <mi>a</mi>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>2</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
          <para><emphasis>Step
        3 - </emphasis>Combine like terms.</para>
          <para>
            <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline">
              <mrow>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>a</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
                <mo>−</mo>
                <mn>6</mn>
                <msup>
                  <mi>b</mi>
                  <mn>3</mn>
                </msup>
              </mrow>
            </math></para>
        </div>
      </div>
      <!--End
      interaction-->
    </section>
  </content>
  <glossary>
    <definition id="fs-id1167836508735">
      <term>binomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167836508741">A binomial is a polynomial with exactly two terms.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167836508745">
      <term>degree of a constant</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167836508750">The degree of any constant is 0.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167836508755">
      <term>degree of a polynomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167836508760">The degree of a polynomial is the highest degree of all its
        terms.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167836508764">
      <term>degree of a term</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167833385664">The degree of a term is the sum of the exponents of its
        variables.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167833385668">
      <term>monomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167833385674">A monomial is an algebraic expression with one term. A
        monomial in one variable is a term of the form <m:math>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>a</m:mi>
            <m:msup>
              <m:mi>x</m:mi>
              <m:mi>m</m:mi>
            </m:msup>
            <m:mo>,</m:mo>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math> where <emphasis effect="italics">a</emphasis>
        is a constant and <emphasis effect="italics">m</emphasis> is a whole number.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167829853744">
      <term>polynomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167829853749">A monomial or two or more monomials combined by addition or
        subtraction is a polynomial.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167829853754">
      <term>standard form of a polynomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167829853760">A polynomial is in standard form when the terms of a
        polynomial are written in descending order of degrees.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167833350539">
      <term>trinomial</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167833350544">A trinomial is a polynomial with exactly three terms.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="fs-id1167833350548">
      <term>polynomial function</term>
      <meaning id="fs-id1167833350554">A polynomial function is a function whose range values are
        defined by a polynomial.</meaning>
    </definition>
  </glossary>
</document>